        abstract:
          "Kaduna Metropolis presents a linguistically varied and socially layered setting where language functions not only as a means of exchange but also as a central marker of self-definition. This study examines how multilingual youths draw on Hausa, English, Nigerian Pidgin, and other available codes to shape and reshape their identities within an environment marked by shifting cultural expectations. There search shows that language choice operates as an intentional act through which young people position themselves in relation to ethnicity, religion, class, and their own sense of participation in wider global currents. Their movement across codes is not a sign of linguistic limitation. Instead, it reflects a skilled practice that allows them to manage varied social ties and to craft identities that are flexible rather than fixed. These switching patterns reveal creative efforts to balance inherited traditions with changing urban values. They also demonstrate how youths assert a measure of control over how they are viewed by peers, families, and institutions. By observing these practices, the study highlights the ways in which multilingual repertoires become instruments of social agency. This offers a clearer understanding of how identity is formed, negotiated, and sometimes contested in a fast growing African city where multiple languages meet and interact. The findings suggest that the linguistic behavior of Kaduna youths provides a meaningful window into the broader processes that shape modern urban life in Northern Nigeria. Keywords: Multilingualism, Identity Negotiation, Urban Youth, Kaduna Metropolis, Sociolinguistics, Code switching, Hybridity.",
