          "Building on my previous work that illustrated the dynamic and diverse ethno-regional identities in Nigeria's colonial army informing the flexibility of British \"martial race\" stereotypes, this paper focuses on the enlistment of men from beyond the territory. In the context of almost non-existent borders and established labor migration patterns, many men from the adjacent French-ruled territories of Niger and Chad as well as German-ruled and later mandated Cameroon joined the army in colonial Nigeria. While British officials disapproved of this process as it hindered their planned development of an army reserve, manpower challenges meant that such enlistments were hard to stop. Furthermore, small but significant numbers of men from more far-flung territories including Senegal, Guinea-Bissau and Liberia enlisted in the colonial army in Nigeria. Indeed, the Nigeria Marine, one of the only locally based colonial navies in Africa, was initially composed of Liberian sailors and transitioned to men from the Niger Delta. And Nigeria was not just a destination for transnational military enlistment as some Nigerians joined other colonial armies such as the Force Publique in the Congo Free State of the 1880s and 1890s. The paper shows that transnational military enlistment continued into the post-colonial period with, for instance, many Chadians joining the Nigerian Army during the Civil War (1967-70). The legacy of this colonial practice also arguably applies to men from other countries linking up with Nigeria's more recent criminal and insurgent groups. Overall, the paper uses the Nigerian case as an example of the transnational character of military service in colonial Africa and discusses its legacy. Keywords: Military History, Enlistment, Transnational, Colonialism, Nigeria Regiment, Nigeria Marine",
