        abstract:
          "The hegemony of Western positivist science within the global intellectual enterprise has long rendered alternative epistemologies invisible or pathological, as the latter is labelled 'primitive', 'unscientific', or, in the case of Africa, 'Negative'. This study employs the philosophical framework of Criticology a meta-theory grounded in the critique of dominant knowledge systems and the validation of subaltern thought to dismantle this epistemic hierarchy. We argue that what has been pejoratively termed African 'Negative' Science is not an absence of science, but a sophisticated, relational, and process-oriented epistemological tradition. Juxtaposed against the reductionist, objectifying, and substance-oriented thrust of Western positivism, African 'Negative' Science offers a disruptive and vital paradigm. It prioritizes interconnectedness (internexism), vitalist causality, ethical accountability, and knowledge-as-praxis. By situating this indigenous framework within Criticology, this study seeks to liberate African thought from the confines of Western epistemological validation and posit it as a crucial, complementary, and corrective modality of knowing essential for addressing complex global challenges, from ecological crises to the erosion of community. Keywords: Criticology, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Negative Science, Epistemic Justice, Relationality.",
