          'The contemporary landscape of knowledge is characterized by unprecedented complexity and interconnectivity, demanding analytical frameworks that transcend traditional disciplinary boundaries. While interdisciplinary is widely advocated, the methodologies for rigorous, constructive critique across these boundaries remain underdeveloped. This study proposes "Criticology" as a systematic framework for interdisciplinary subject-matter critique. Criticology is defined as the meta-disciplinary study and application of the principles, protocols, and practices of effective critique. It moves beyond ad-hoc peer review by offering a shared conceptual scaffold for evaluating claims, methods, and knowledge structures irrespective of their domain of origin. The study first establishes the theoretical necessity of Criticology, situating it within philosophies of science, critical theory, and systems thinking. It then delineates the framework\'s core components: its foundational axioms, a phased process model for critique (the "Criticological Cycle"), and a set of transversal analytical lenses. Through illustrative case studies spanning the sciences, social sciences, and humanities, we demonstrate Criticology\'s utility in identifying epistemic gaps, challenging unstated assumptions, and fostering generative interdisciplinary dialogue. The study concludes by addressing potential objections and outlining the framework\'s implications for academic practice, peer review, and pedagogy, arguing that Criticology is a vital tool for navigating the 21st-century epistemological terrain. Keywords: Criticology, Interdisciplinarity, Epistemology, Knowledge Integration, Systems Thinking.',
