        title:
          "BREWING BELONGING: THE ROLE OF BEER IN RITUAL AND IDENTITY FORMATION AMONG THE GOEMAI PEOPLE OF CENTRAL NIGERIA",
        author: "Changwak Emmanuel Jonah, Jatau David Polang, PhD",
        abstract:
          "The paper examines the cultural significance of traditional beer in the rituals and identity formation of the Goemai people of Central Nigeria. Among the Goemai, locally brewed beer commonly known a mues is more than a mere local beverage, it functions as a vital social and ritualistic medium that fosters communal cohension, expresses social values and negotiates individual and collective identities. Through oral sources, ethnographic research and analysis of ritual practices such as festivals, marriage ceremonies, naming ceremonies, communal labour, relaxation and ancestral veneration, this study reveals how beer rituals serve to reinforce social hierarchies, gender roles and intergenerational ties. The communal brewing and sharing of mues during key events act as a symbolic act of belonging and cultural continuity, mediating relationship between individuals, families and the wider community. By situating beer at the intersection of ritual practice and identity construction, the paper will contribute to the broader discussions on the role alcohol, intoxication and relaxation as a popular culture in the 21st century. Keywords: Brewing, Beer, Alcohol, Ritual, Goemai, Central Nigeria.",
