        abstract:
          "Apart from its primary entertainment value, the instruments, melodies and lyrics of music have made it a tool for cultural preservation, storytelling, conscientization, activism and identification especially in heterogeneous milieus. In recent times, music has emerged as a tool for political mobilization. Between the year 2001 and 2023, election seasons in Jos-North LGA and Plateau State in general were characterized by politically themed music composed by musicians for various political parties to mobilize political supporters. Scholars have however not considered the dynamics and relevance of such music in political mobilization in the politics of Jos. This study seeks to interrogate the role of music as an identity marker and its utility in political mobilization in the politics of Jos-North LGA between 2001-2023. This qualitative study primarily employs the historical research methodology but also borrows the anthropological methods. Primary sources such as oral interviews, recorded music and observations were used. Secondary sources such as published and unpublished books, journals, theses and dissertations were also used. The thematic and chronological methods of analysis were used. The study reveals that there is a nexus between music and politics in Jos-North LGAand that politically themed music were markers of political identities in the politics of Jos-North and played an important role in the mobilization of political supporters and voters, influencing their political actions. The study suggests that music could be utilized as a tool for social integration and achievement of peace in the area. Keywords: Music, Identities, Politics, Political Mobilization, Development",
