        author: "Atando Dauda Agbu, Kenda Peter Nwhesire",
        abstract:
          "Leisure, recreation and entertainment activities are integral aspects of human life right from when man began to live in an organized society, which have remained important components of the way of life of the African people. Though, before the 21st century, historians showed less interest in the study and documentation of the social history of the African societies, which leisure, recreation and entertainment are integral parts, and so could not explain the role or functions of these activities in societal development in general, and the contributions of women in them and their benefits. Interestingly, with social history attaining global relevance in the 21st century, historians have intensified their focus on the study and understanding of not only on the role of leisure in the development of the African people and societies, but also on the contributions of women in the enterprise. This is despite strong constraints, including gender issues. This study, proposes to investigate the contributions and constraints of women in the leisure, recreation and entertainment activities in Taraba State of Nigeria, using historical approach, deriving information from oral and secondary sources. The findings of this study would be of great source of encouragement, not only to African women, in their participation in these activities, but also provide better information to policy makers in understanding the importance of these activities for the purpose of making policies that would support the industry. It would also be of importance to researchers on the subject matter, particularly, and social history in general. Keywords: Development, entertainment, gender, leisure, recreation & well-being.",
