        title:
          "COLONIALISM, CHRISTIANITY AND IDENTITY FORMATION IN THE JOS PLATEAU AREA OF BRITISH NIGERIA",
        author: "Dr. Samuel Sani Abdullahi",
        abstract:
          "Many people indigenous to the Jos Plateau area of Nigeria identify as Christians. The influence of Christianity on local identity in the Jos Plateau highlands and surrounding low lands is traced to its introduction to the area shortly after the subjugation and incorporation of the people of the area, by the British, into the northern part of the colony of Nigeria during the early part of the 20th century. Hence, the extension of colonial rule over the Jos Plateau area shaped the experiences of the Christian religious encounter of the local population. For example, Christianity was conveyed along with European cultural baggage. Such baggage include European notions of racial superiority and colonially instituted local cultural hierarchies that arranged members of different language groups according to some sought of stratification. Members of some language groups indigenous to the area were placed at the lower part of the scale of the colonial stratification of local cultural groups. However, people indigenous to the Jos Plateau area challenged their dual subordination using various avenues including the deployment of their new found Christian identity. Thus, the local assimilation of Christianity became a valuable cultural currency that was appropriated to navigate the colonial space. This paper, therefore, aims to examine how this Christian sense of belonging in the Jos Plateau area emerged, in an area that has been identified as a centre of cultural fragmentation, within the complexities of British rule in Northern Nigeria. Keywords: Colonialism, Christianity, Identity, Jos-Plateau",
