        abstract:
          "Zazzau Emirate, one of Northern Nigeria's oldest and most expansive traditional polities, has long served as a microcosm of the nation's plural tapestry. Stretching across a mosaic of ethnic, cultural and religious communities, its strategic geography and cosmopolitan character has forged a uniquely accommodating society. Yet the emirate's history is punctuated by periodic crises communal clashes, ethno-religious tensions, and spill-over conflicts from neighbouring regions that threaten to destabilise not only Zazzau but the wider northern corridor.Based on the use of archival records, oral histories, and contemporary interviews, this study traces the emirate's peace-building interventions from the post-civil-war era (1975) to the present day. It interrogates how successive Zazzau traditional rulers through symbolic mediation, customary dispute-resolution mechanisms, and strategic alliances with state and religious actors contained outbreaks of violence and prevented their escalation beyond emirate borders. Key episodes examined include the 1992 inter-communal riots, the 2009 Boko Haram incursion, and the 2022 farmer-herder flashpoints. The analysis reveals three interlocking pillars of the emirate's peace architecture: legitimacy, rooted in centuries-old customary law; networks that link grassroots leaders, religious scholars, and government officials; and adaptability in blending traditional mediation with modern conflict-resolution tools. The studyconcludes with actionable recommendations such as institutionalising joint peace-monitoring committees, leveraging youth-led initiatives, and integrating emirate-based early-warning systems into state security frameworks to ensure that peace and harmony can coexist sustainably within Zazzau's diverse society and beyond. Keywords: Role Of Traditional Rulers, Peace Building, Dispute Resolution And Zazzau Emirate",
